    "nexus_regulate.json",        # INTEGRATION (nexus_regulate.py, 'mutreg' syscall): wire NEXUS (protein-mutation health) into the regulation stack -- the two layers meet at the TF (a TF is a protein). NEXUS turns (ddG_fold, ddG_bind) into a graded ACTIVITY (soft-AND of folded x bound fractions, validated near-field ~0.5 Pearson/0.77 hotspot AUC; LOF or GOF); the TF's curated direct regulon (TRRUST signed) then moves first-order: target change = sign*(activity-1), so an activated target follows activity and a repressed target opposes it. Demoed: GATA1 severe fold ddG=7.5 -> activity 0.31 (LOF) -> 28 signed targets move (EPO/HEMGN/CEBPA down, repressed targets up); mild ddG=2 -> activity ~1 -> nothing moves (correct: folding is conservative, proteins are marginally stable). HONEST: composes two SEPARATELY-validated layers (NEXUS near-field structure + curated direct regulon binding->regulation 2.3x) into the FIRST-ORDER regulatory consequence of a TF mutation; does NOT predict the genome-wide downstream cascade (the dynamics wall measured all session); extrinsic sensor needs a KNOWN protein interface (folding is general). A mechanistic mutation->direct-regulation chain, bounded at the direct regulon               # PRODUCT (regulate.py, 'regulate' syscall; data via fetch_invivo.py): the honest test of the user's thesis "binding != regulation, UNLESS it recruits polymerase". A TF sitting on DNA is occupancy; regulation is occupancy productive enough to appoint RNA Pol II and fire transcription. Tests, on REAL K562 data vs the CRISPR ground truth (ENCFF968BZL = harmonised Nasser2021/Gasperini2019/Schraivogel2020 enhancer-perturbation; Significant = silencing the element truly changes a gene = the operational definition of regulation), whether the PRODUCTIVITY signature separates real regulators from merely open/marked elements. Marks: POLR2A + POLR2AphosphoS5 (Pol II recruited/initiating, Ser5P), PRO-cap BIDIRECTIONAL peaks (eRNA = the enhancer is actually being transcribed). MEASURED, element-level (3,961 CRISPR-tested elements, base rate regulator 12.5%): productivity ladder open 14.4% -> +active/H3K27ac 20.0% -> +Pol II 27.6% -> +eRNA 28.9% (2.3x base) -- recruiting polymerase + firing eRNA MORE THAN DOUBLES the odds an element is a real regulator. SHARP TEST (among open+active H3K27ac+ elements): eRNA firing 26.0% vs silent 12.9% (= base rate) -- i.e. the H3K27ac MARK alone barely discriminates; the ACT of transcription (Pol II + eRNA) is what separates regulation from decoration, exactly the user's point. FULL CHAIN (pair-level, 10,331 element->gene pairs, base 5.5% Significant; Gate3 which-gene x Gate4 productivity): productive AND ABC-3D-linked-to-that-gene 31.4% (5.7x base), neither 2.4% (below base = non-regulating). So binding that recruits Pol II AND loops to the gene is ~6x more likely to be REAL regulation. HONEST: a strong MEASURED discriminator, NOT deterministic -- precision ceilings ~30% because (a) CRISPR power limits (underpowered true pairs labelled not-significant), (b) enhancer REDUNDANCY/shadow enhancers (silencing one productive enhancer doesn't move the gene if another compensates), (c) ChIP occupancy is a population proxy for residence-time x concentration, NOT single-molecule dwell time (no genome-wide dwell-time data exists -- the actual 'binds long enough' cause is not directly measurable). Measures the MARGINAL value of productivity on accessibility-preselected elements. This is the honest bridge from BINDING (invivo_gate) toward REGULATION: the productivity signal genuinely enriches regulation ~6x, but does not make it certain. All real K562 data; the answer is measured, not asserted
    "propagate.json",              # INTEGRATION (propagate.py, 'propagate'/'blastradius' syscall): the FORWARD multi-layer propagation the network was missing -- mutate ANY protein/gene (NEXUS as the entry point) and flow the effect layer by layer: L0 NEXUS activity (soft-AND fold x bind, LOF/GOF) -> L1 regulation (if a TF: its TRRUST-signed direct regulon is the first affected set + direction) -> L2 products/PPI/complex (physical partners of the seed) -> L3 pathways (Reactome pathways the affected proteins sit in) -> L4 crosstalk (pathways sharing >=3 proteins with a hit pathway). TRANSCRIPTION RATE (the user's point 'don't forget how promoter and TF regulate the rate'): L1 targets are ordered by a mechanistic rate model dRate = sign*(activity-1) x promoter-PolII (POLR2A ChIP at the target's TSS+-1kb) -- the PROMOTER sets the baseline transcription rate, the TF MODULATES it; direction = activated target FOLLOWS the TF (GATA1 LOF -> CDC6/BST2 down), repressed target is RELIEVED (GATA1 LOF -> CBFB/SPI1 up, matches measured Perturb-seq z: CBFB +1.08, SPI1 +1.85). HONEST FRAMING (measured, validate()): this is a STRUCTURAL forward-reachability / blast-radius map -- WHAT is mechanistically connected to the mutation and COULD move -- NOT the quantitative dynamical cascade (which genes actually move, by how much; that does not compose -- graph propagation ~chance, knockout recall 3-18%, the session's dynamics wall). validate() quantifies the map-vs-simulator boundary on GATA1's real K562 Perturb-seq movers: L1 regulon reach 29 precision 0.069 = 9.2x chance (enriched), L2 +PPI/complex reach 631 = 2.1x, L3 +pathway members reach 5183 = 1.08x (diluted to chance as the radius balloons). RATE caveat: the model captures the MECHANISM (promoter baseline x TF modulation x sign) and its DIRECTION agrees with measured better than chance (5/8 signed GATA1 targets), but its MAGNITUDES do NOT match measured effect sizes (r~0) -- the per-TF->target rate COEFFICIENT (how strongly THIS TF drives THAT promoter) is the unmeasured quantity, so direction is more reliable than rate. STILL MISSING (acknowledged, not built): splicing, 5' capping, poly-A tail, full epigenetic state -- each its own hard problem. A map of the POSSIBLE (mechanistically-connected blast radius, honestly bounded), not a simulator of the ACTUAL. PRECISION FIX (rank_targets/validate_ranked): the flat radius has good recall but ~chance precision because it is UNRANKED (all reachable genes equal). Fix = a single RANKED, CALIBRATED score composite = 10*regulon*(0.5+promoter_rate) + RWR_nearfield (random-walk-with-restart over the directed regulatory + PPI + complex graph; pathway co-membership DROPPED as it was the dilution source). MEASURED on GATA1's real movers (leakage-controlled): composite AUPRC 2.32x base, top decile 2.41x, P@10 0.10 (~12x); beats degree (0.99x=chance), promoter-rate-alone (1.16x), RWR-alone (1.71x); label-shuffle real 2.32x vs null 1.17x p=0.013; robust across |z| thresholds (1.86/1.97/2.32 at 2/2.5/3). It RANKS the radius so the TOP is usable but does NOT make the far field precise -- GATA1's real movers are a de-repressed secondary program (all UP) the curated edges don't connect to (SPI1/CEBPA relay 0x) = the wall located at the missing EDGES, not the algorithm
    "capping.json",                # PRODUCT (capping.py, 'cap' syscall; FIRST_PRINCIPLES.md sec1): 5' m7G cap as a PROVENANCE-GATED machinery layer, not sequence-regulated. Unlike splicing/poly-A (where the RNA sequence is the signal), a transcript is capped iff it is a Serine5-phosphorylated Pol II product AND the capping machinery is intact -- so cap status is a near-constitutive Boolean (Ser5P-Pol-II provenance at the TSS x machinery integrity) that GATES cap-dependent stability/eIF4E-translation. The testable FIRST-PRINCIPLES prediction is the obligatory-role hierarchy cap0 > cap1 > cap2, derived purely from each worker's role and VALIDATED against measured DepMap dep_frac: RNGTT (triphosphatase+guanylyltransferase) 1.0 and RNMT (N7-MTase) 0.97 build cap0, obligatory for eIF4E -> lethal HUBS; CMTR1 (cap1 2'-O-MTase) 0.85 = important (innate-immune self-mark + translation); CMTR2 (cap2) 0.04 = dispensable MODIFIER. Monotone cap0>cap1>cap2 confirmed; CBC reader NCBP1/2 and eIF4E all essential. NEXUS on a worker: hub LoF (RNGTT/RNMT) = GLOBAL cap-dependent-translation collapse (short-half-life/5'TOP transcripts first; measured-lethal), modifier LoF (CMTR2) = viable subset + innate-immune 'non-self' flag (IFIT1/RIG-I). HONEST: cap PRESENCE on an ordinary Pol II mRNA is deterministic (don't predict from sequence); the per-transcript cap0:cap1:cap2 methylation fractions and decap/recap homeostasis pool need cap-specific sequencing. Direction/essentiality reliable, methylation-state fractions measurement-only
    "polya.json",                  # PRODUCT (polya.py, 'polya'/'apa' syscall; FIRST_PRINCIPLES.md sec2): 3' cleavage & polyadenylation + alternative polyadenylation. The machine reads a cis-element BARCODE and commits an irreversible cut ~10-30nt downstream of an AAUAAA hexamer. SITE STRENGTH is sequence-computable (pas_strength = tiered hexamer AATAAA/ATTAAA/variants x position-weight peaking ~-21nt, + GU/U downstream element, + upstream UGUA/CFIm, + CA cut) and VALIDATED against real transcript 3' ends fetched from Ensembl: on 20 genes a canonical/variant hexamer sits in the -40..-1 upstream window 85% of the time vs 0% in a downstream control (34x, 0.5 pseudocount), MEDIAN position -22nt (textbook ~-21) -- the machinery's recognition signal is exactly where first principles says. The APA RATIO (proximal vs distal 3'UTR choice) is CELL-STATE, not sequence: set by CFIm25(NUDT21)/CstF64(CSTF2) abundance (this cell's measured ppm ratio 115/13.6 = 8.5 -> distal/long-3'UTR bias) + Pol II elongation speed -- sequence gives the menu and intrinsic strengths, the cell picks the ratio (Masamha 2014: CFIm25 knockdown shortens UTRs, de-represses oncogenes). Chosen 3'UTR -> miRNA-seed + ARE(AUUUA) load -> stability DIRECTION for the decay layer (shorter UTR = fewer repressive sites = more stable). HONEST: site strength + variant direction (stronger/weaker site, longer/shorter UTR) reliable; cell-type APA ratio and tail-length->half-life need 3'-seq/TAIL-seq (tail length is not even a clean monotonic half-life predictor -- Lima 2017)
    "epigenetics.json",            # PRODUCT (epigenetics.py, 'epi'/'chromatin' syscall; FIRST_PRINCIPLES.md sec4): the EPIGENETICS layer (B) as three tractable pieces with the honest three-tier ceiling. (a) STATIC ChromHMM-style chromatin-state vector per promoter from 6 ENCODE K562 marks (H3K4me3/me1, H3K27ac, H3K27me3, H3K9me3, DNase read at TSS+-2kb via crispr_gate._max_signal) -> label {active/bivalent/polycomb/heterochromatin/primed/quiescent}. VALIDATED (a labeling of measured data): state predicts measured K562 expression (RNAdecayCafe RPKM) -- active promoters median log10 RPKM +1.08 vs BIVALENT -1.00 (~100x lower), bivalent 4.07x enriched for silencing (base silent 0.14); H3K27me3-presence AUC-for-silent 0.57 (weak alone, the CLASS discriminates). (c) mutate_writer = the epigenetic analogue of nexus_regulate.mutate_tf: a writer/eraser mutation -> NEXUS graded activity -> its DIRECT targets are the loci carrying its mark (EZH2->H3K27me3 loci; loss->de-repression). VALIDATED first-order DIRECTION vs real Perturb-seq WITH a specificity control: EZH2 KD -> H3K27me3-marked genes 5.33x enriched among top-200 up-movers (delta mean z +0.069), which CLEANLY BEATS all non-writer control KDs (MYC 0.95/SPI1 0.76/TP53 1.14/GATA1 2.86/RPL13 1.71, max 2.86x); the accessory PRC2 subunits EED (1.53x) and SUZ12 (2.48x) fall WITHIN the control range -- honestly reported as PRC2 redundancy + the wall. HONEST 3-tier ceiling (same shape as the knockout-cascade wall): static state KNOWABLE (measured labeling); writer->direct-target FIRST-ORDER/DIRECTION-ONLY (enriched but weak, delta z ~0.07, magnitudes unmeasured); genome-wide chromatin DECOMPACTION CASCADE does NOT compose -- first-principles reason: read-write feedback makes marks bistable/hysteretic (losing one writer often does nothing, surviving marks re-propagate) + enzyme redundancy (EZH1/EZH2, DNMT3A/3B). Tracks fetched: ENCFF403DTU/486QGD/578EES/963GZJ (H3K4me3/me1, H3K27me3, H3K9me3)
    "halflife.json",               # PRODUCT (halflife.py, 'halflife'/'decay' syscall; FIRST_PRINCIPLES.md sec3): the mRNA-DECAY layer (A4), tested HONESTLY against measured half-lives -- the first post-transcriptional layer where LOSING WAS POSSIBLE, and it predicts. Predicts per-gene ln(k_deg) in K562 from SEQUENCE (log-additive first-principles features: 61-codon composition -> codon optimality learned IN-FOLD [no CSC leakage], 3'UTR length/ARE-AUUUA/GC/m6A-DRACH, 5'UTR length/uAUG, CDS GC) vs RNAdecayCafe measured half-lives (Zenodo 15785218, uniformly-reprocessed pulse-label SLAM/TimeLapse-seq; K562 = our cell line, 10,802 genes; ground truth = avg_log_kdeg). CHROMOSOME-GroupKFold (paralog-safe), 8,556 expressed genes. RESULT: full linear ridge R2 0.279 / pearson 0.529 / spearman 0.526; SAME features non-linear XGBoost R2 0.327 / r 0.572 / rho 0.558. CODON OPTIMALITY is the dominant single signal (codon-only R2 0.199 vs 3'UTR-length-only 0.068) -- exactly as first principles predicts (the biggest CDS-encoded determinant). Top features: CDS GC (+, faster decay), 3'UTR length (+, longer=faster decay=more repressive sites). This is the layer that turns a transcription-RATE change into a steady-state mRNA LEVEL ([mRNA]_ss = k_txn/k_deg). HONEST: a first-principles FLOOR (interpretable linear/GBM on hand-built features), not the ceiling -- Saluki (Agarwal & Kelley 2022, deep CNN+RNN on full sequence) reaches R2~0.5-0.6 but only on DENOISED multi-dataset CONSENSUS; on a single cell line the achievable ceiling is ~0.35-0.45, so our r~0.57 is genuinely competitive. Sequence predicts ranking/direction (rho 0.56); absolute half-life needs measured decay rates. A REAL held-out quantitative prediction against SLAM-seq, not confirmatory
    "protein_abundance.json",      # PRODUCT (protein_abundance.py, 'pabund'/'concentration' syscall): the missing lifecycle segment mRNA-level -> PROTEIN concentration, tested held-out vs MEASURED protein ppm. The user's chain [protein]=k_translation*[mRNA]/k_protein_decay: does modeling translation (production) + degradation from sequence beat mRNA-alone? Target = log10 measured protein ppm; base = measured K562 log mRNA (RNAdecayCafe RPKM); features = translation proxies (61-codon composition/optimality, 5'UTR len/uORF/GC, CDS len) + degradation proxies (protein length, N-end-rule N-terminal class, PEST fraction, disorder-prone fraction, hydrophobic fraction from the translated CDS). Chromosome-GroupKFold, 7,515 genes. RESULT: mRNA-only r 0.594 (R2 0.352) -> +translation r 0.708 (R2 0.501) -> +degradation r 0.610 -> full XGBoost r 0.734 (R2 0.538). TRANSLATION (production) DOMINATES (+0.11 over mRNA); protein DEGRADATION adds little (+0.02) -- protein turnover is degron/context-specific, barely sequence-predictable, exactly as Schwanhausser 2011 found (mRNA explains only ~40% of protein; translation is the bigger determinant; protein half-life uncorrelated with mRNA half-life). CLOSES the abundance lifecycle: transcription rate -> mRNA level (r~0.57, halflife.py) -> protein level (r~0.73). STEP 5 check (does abundance predict a knockout's importance?): abundance vs essentiality dep_frac spearman +0.348 (essential proteins median log-abundance 1.03 vs -0.59 = ~40x more abundant), on par with network degree (+0.366) -- so abundance is a MODEST real predictor of a knockout's SEED importance, but NOT the genome-wide cascade (that stays the measured wall). HONEST: absolute protein concentration in a given state needs measured proteomics (ppm, already in the image); downstream abundance-weighting USES the measurement, this validates the production->concentration mechanism
    "kinetics.json",               # RESEARCH (kinetics.py, 'kinetics' syscall): the kinetic-competition model of productive initiation (the user's idea: a bound TF races -- fall off at k_off vs hold long enough to appoint Pol II at k_init; P(productive)=tau_res/(tau_res+tau_appoint)) and an HONEST MEASURED test of it. Built faithfully: PWM log-odds -> RELATIVE binding energy dDDG/kT vs consensus (Berg-von Hippel biophysical PWM) -> under the diffusion-limited (k_on const) assumption tau_res=tau_max*exp(-dDDG) -> kinetic competition with literature order-of-magnitude tau_max~12s (SMT specific-site residence) and tau_appoint~30s (Pol II/PIC assembly). THE DEEP CATCH (the crux): affinity is THERMODYNAMIC (Kd=k_off/k_on), residence time is KINETIC (1/k_off); a PWM gives equilibrium energy->Kd and says NOTHING about k_off alone -- two sites can share a Kd yet have 100x different residence. Turning affinity into residence REQUIRES assuming k_on const, exactly what the single-molecule field contests; true k_off needs single-molecule tracking (SMT), per-TF not genome-wide. MEASURED TEST on 2,101 real K562 ChIP-bound motif sites (10 TFs, chr22) of the model's central prediction (higher affinity => longer residence => more likely to win the race => more Pol II/eRNA): SEQUENCE AFFINITY is FLAT vs productivity (pooled r(PolII)=+0.017, r(eRNA)=-0.013; affinity quartiles show no monotonic rise), but MEASURED OCCUPANCY (ChIP signalValue, a direct readout) DOES predict productivity (r(PolII)=+0.205, r(eRNA)=+0.292). THE PRECISE CONCLUSION: the mechanism is RIGHT -- occupancy/residence genuinely predicts polymerase recruitment (r~0.2-0.3) -- but the link from SEQUENCE/affinity to occupancy/residence is BROKEN, because Kd != k_off and because functional enhancers famously use deliberately LOW-affinity sites (affinity is decoupled from function by design). So the k_off!=Kd wall is now MEASURED, not just argued: you can write the rate equation and even confirm occupancy drives productivity, but you CANNOT fill in residence time from the motif -- it must be measured (SMT), which has no genome-wide assay. The model output is a RELATIVE propensity (order-of-magnitude params), not an absolute rate. This locates the wall exactly at sequence->residence, one layer deeper than 'binding vs regulation'. CLOSING THE LOOP (added): plugged MEASURED single-molecule (SMT) residence times into the competition model and tested ACROSS TFs vs measured productivity -- it does NOT close the loop. The CTCF natural experiment is decisive: CTCF has a robustly-measured LONG residence (~90s, Hansen 2017), so the model predicts P(productive)=0.75 (highest of all TFs), but CTCF's measured eRNA+ is only 0.032 (near LOWEST) because its long residence is ARCHITECTURAL (insulator/loop anchor), not activating. Cross-TF r(measured residence, eRNA)=-0.32 (NEGATIVE) -- though this leans on the CTCF measured point (8/10 non-CTCF TFs share a class-typical ~10s placeholder since TF-specific K562 SMT doesn't exist for them), so the claim rests on CTCF + biology, not a 10-point regression. THE RESOLUTION: the causal residence->output law IS real but is INTRA-TF / per-SITE -- proven for the SAME factor varying its own site/condition (Gal4 high->low affinity site reduced residence AND burst duration; GR residence correlates with burst size; p53 residence correlates with its transcriptional activity) -- it does NOT transfer across TFs of different function class, and the per-site in-vivo residence that would test it genome-wide has no assay. So even MEASURED residence can't be scaled into a genome-wide productivity predictor with existing data: the mechanism the user proposed is correct and experimentally proven, but only in the regime where it's been measured (one factor, its own sites), which can't be scaled up. A genuinely honest negative that pinpoints the missing measurement (per-site in-vivo residence / SMT genome-wide)
